00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.840
Now, back to boring large language models.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.520
When it comes to large language&nbsp;
models, we have been bored lately.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:38.560
We have been bored by GLaM from&nbsp;
Google AI which has a total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:43.920
of 1.2 trillion parameters,&nbsp;
but don’t be that impressed!

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:49.120
The model works a lot with&nbsp;
sparsity, so it activates only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:56.160
97 billion parameters at a time, which&nbsp;
is 8% of the capacity of the whole model.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:03.120
And on the sparse model landscape with trillions&nbsp;
of parameters, we could also count Wu Dao 2.0&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:11.440
of 1.75 trillion parameters or Google’s Switch&nbsp;
Transformers of 1.6 trillion parameters.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:17.680
But let’s take a look at dense models, shall we?&nbsp;
We have been bothered by LaMDA from Google AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:23.200
which is a 137 billion parameter&nbsp;
model specialized on dialog.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:29.280
Then with Gopher from DeepMind&nbsp;
of 280 billion parameters.&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.200
And let’s not forget the&nbsp;
largest monolithic model so far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:41.120
called Megatron-Turing NLG from Microsoft&nbsp;
and NVIDIA with 530 billion parameters.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:44.640
Ok, and of course Google needed&nbsp;
to claim the biggest model again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:51.120
so they made PaLM, just a bit bigger, just enough&nbsp;
to overshadow Megatron by 10 billion parameters.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:56.240
So yeah, they made a 540 billion&nbsp;
parameter model. Keep in mind that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:02.720
this model is dense, all parameters are used&nbsp;
during inference, there is no sparsity here.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:05.840
In terms of model architecture, PaLM does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.880
innovate, it is a GPT like&nbsp;
dense transformer decoder.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:12.800
The beginning of the text goes in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:17.600
the transformer predicts the next word,&nbsp;
while attending to the previous words.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.600
The authors do use some tricks&nbsp;
for training this beast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:26.240
for example they are computing attention&nbsp;
and the feedworward layer in parallel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:30.480
which is what people from EleutherAI&nbsp;
have been doing with GPT-J-6B.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:38.080
For example, PaLM uses SwiGLU activations, Rotary&nbsp;
Positional Embeddings, and a special “lossless”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:44.000
vocabulary to account for many languages in&nbsp;
the training data, for code, and for numbers.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:50.320
These things have already been proven to help&nbsp;
train large language models by previous work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:55.840
PaLM’s innovation is completely geared&nbsp;
towards training this beast efficiently.&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.480
Now, what does it mean efficiently – for Google?

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.520
It certainly does not mean&nbsp;
the same as it means for you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:10.960
when you are happy you parallelize your GPU&nbsp;
training on your 4 GPUs your cluster node has. ;)

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:18.800
For Google AI, this means – and I hope&nbsp;
you are seated – that they used 6144&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.800
TPU chips for training.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:26.400
Wow. Just for comparison, the most&nbsp;
impressive numbers so far were established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:37.840
for Megatron-Turing NLG on 2240 A100 GPUs and&nbsp;
for Gopher which went up to 4096 TPU v3 chips.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:46.320
So how does it work, how can Ms. Coffee Bean&nbsp;
train on 6144 TPU chips too? Well, she can’t,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:56.400
but Google could because they used two TPU v4 pods&nbsp;
with 3072 chips each with fast intercommunication.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:02.240
Each pod should have a full copy of the model&nbsp;
parameters partitioned over the 3072 chips.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:09.040
Now, to let the two pods communicate efficiently,&nbsp;
the authors used the Pathways system which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:14.320
introduced by Google in a previous paper.&nbsp;
In a nutshell, a batch of training data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:20.880
is divided into half and each half is sent to&nbsp;
each pod for forward and backward propagation.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:26.160
Then the gradients on half the batch are&nbsp;
transferred to the other pod and vice-versa.

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:32.000
But this also means that the transfer must&nbsp;
wait until the other pod finishes and when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:38.720
the waiting is complete, a burst in data transfer&nbsp;
of 81 Terrabytes per second happens at a time.

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:43.920
But you can imagine that all this waiting means&nbsp;
that in this time, the cores cannot really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:49.600
compute interesting stuff. Really impressive&nbsp;
is that through a lot of engineering tricks Ms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:56.800
Coffee Bean has no idea about, they achieved an&nbsp;
analytically computed hardware FLOPs utilization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:13.040
of over 50%, which is impressive and&nbsp;
unprecedented. In this table comparing PaLM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:21.120
to previous large language models, you can see a&nbsp;
FLOPs utilization of 46.2%, so smaller than 57.8%.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:27.440
This is because in the table, you can&nbsp;
find the FLOPs utilization without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:35.040
rematerialisation. Rematerialisation means&nbsp;
that one saves memory by not storing some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:39.120
intermediate activations, but&nbsp;
re-computing them when needed.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:46.160
This means that with rematerialization,&nbsp;
the chips are busier when training PaLM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:51.440
than the other models which are not&nbsp;
using the trick, so for comparability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:55.520
you can see the number in the&nbsp;
table without rematerialization.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:57.520
We see how high this is nonetheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:02.400
a lot higher than for the comparably&nbsp;
inefficient GPT-3 model for example.

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:09.440
Okay, we get it. This thing is big, it’s&nbsp;
training fast, so what is it eating, exactly?

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:17.760
Well, it is feeding of lots and lots of training&nbsp;
data. On 780 billion tokens, to be exact.

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:23.280
50% of the data consists of social media&nbsp;
conversations in multiple languages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:30.000
almost a quarter of the data consists of filtered&nbsp;
webpages, then books, Wikipedia, news and Code.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:35.120
So already from the training data, we assume&nbsp;
that PaLM will have multilingual abilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:38.160
and will be able to do code completion: it used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:48.000
196GB of source code in Java, HTML,&nbsp;
Javascript, Python, PHP, C#, XML, C++, and C.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:52.080
And surprise, surprise, the&nbsp;
model can do code completion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:57.600
fix compilation errors, translate code from&nbsp;
one programming language into the other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:04.480
even though its the pre-training set contained&nbsp;
50 times less Python code than Codex 12B.

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:13.760
The hypothesis here is that PaLM can do&nbsp;
code completion even though only 5% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:20.160
its training data consisted of code, because&nbsp;
it benefits from its size and its training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:27.600
on natural language. This makes it more&nbsp;
data-efficient when it comes to code data.

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:32.800
On natural language PaLM improves&nbsp;
state-of-the-art on common benchmarks, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:38.240
natural language inference, where one has to say&nbsp;
whether a sentence entails or contradicts another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:45.680
reading comprehension, question answering, the&nbsp;
complicated Winograd-style schemas, and so on.

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:50.720
But we did not get to the jaw-dropping&nbsp;
part yet: Google had previously organised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:56.640
the collection of BIG Bench, which stands&nbsp;
for “Beyond the Imitation Game Benchmark”

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:59.520
which contains quite the tricky tasks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:03.440
like delivering persuasive&nbsp;
arguments for false statements,

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:08.160
or finding the Russian proverb closest&nbsp;
in meaning to a given English proverb.

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:12.560
BIG bench is quite the crazy collection&nbsp;
of tasks, where even the average human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:20.160
has a 50% success rate. Well, by scaling PaLM&nbsp;
from 8 billion to 62 billion, to 540 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:25.040
parameters, we see how PaLM in 5-shot&nbsp;
learning, surpasses the average human!

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:30.640
One word here: Maybe you are wondering&nbsp;
what few-shot learning means:

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:37.200
Well, I remember a time when few-shot learning&nbsp;
meant that one trains a model further on 10,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:42.320
maybe a 100 samples and updates the&nbsp;
parameters of the model while doing so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:45.280
with the feedback from the few examples.

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:49.200
Of course, catastrophic forgetting is&nbsp;
a huge problem here and the updates on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:53.280
the little data can undo what a lot&nbsp;
of training have previously done.

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:57.040
But there were parameter&nbsp;
updates through gradients.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:02.480
But since GPT-3, what we have just&nbsp;
described is rather called fine-tuning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:07.120
and the default meaning of “few-shot&nbsp;
learning” has shifted to something else:

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:15.520
The model receives a couple of solved examples&nbsp;
as a prompt which is pre-appended to the input.

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:19.920
So we have problem, solution, problem,&nbsp;
solution, then problem. And when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:24.720
GPT-like model completes the sequence&nbsp;
with the correct examples as input.

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:29.600
Since GPT-3, we have been used to expect&nbsp;
large language models to be sensitive to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:36.080
this kind of prompting and give right answers&nbsp;
even without having any updates done on them.

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:41.200
And we are wondering why this is it called&nbsp;
few-shot learning when there are no updates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:46.960
and there is no feedback loop in case the model&nbsp;
answered incorrectly? Like, where is the learning?

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:50.480
Well, we do not want to start a&nbsp;
discussion about what “learning” means,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:56.000
now. We just wanted to make the point that&nbsp;
few-shot learning as we see it from GPT-3 onwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:58.560
is just glorified pattern completion.

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:02.480
Now, with PaLM in this few-shot&nbsp;
learning setting, there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:06.240
six tasks where we can observe&nbsp;
a discontinuous improvement with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:11.520
scale, so a certain size where the model&nbsp;
suddenly “gets it” with enough parameters.

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:16.080
With enough scale, the capability&nbsp;
of solving the logical sequences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:23.520
just emerges. Is this understanding? I have no&nbsp;
idea, but with English proverbs, Ms. Coffee Bean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:29.840
would assume that enough parameters are enough&nbsp;
to memorize the proverbs? No idea, honestly.

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:33.280
I wonder if there is anybody in&nbsp;
this world understanding what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:35.840
kind of “understanding” happens in these models.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:42.480
We observe these discontinuous jumps, but do&nbsp;
we really know why they happen? Honestly, no.

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:47.120
Speaking of emergence and reasoning, just&nbsp;
look, another emergence from model scale and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:52.880
data scale happens: If one gives a reasoning&nbsp;
example to PaLM, then PaLM reasons too!

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:55.680
The authors call this “chain&nbsp;
of thought prompting”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.240 --> 00:13:01.680
PaLM gets as input a question, its answer,&nbsp;
and a reason. Then for the next question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:06.800
the model will follow the pattern and give&nbsp;
a reason to its answer too! Incredible.

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:13.600
Following the same idea of prompting where the&nbsp;
output is a completion of the given examples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:18.560
PaLM can also explain jokes.&nbsp;
But keep in mind, it can do so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:23.360
because its input is an example of&nbsp;
an explained joke and a new joke.

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:29.440
This makes the joke explanation example in&nbsp;
the blog which was circulated on social media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:34.560
a little bit misleading because it omits to show&nbsp;
the prompt. You decide how much cherry-picking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:40.240
has gone into these examples, because we know&nbsp;
from playing with GPT-3 like models that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:45.840
sometimes succeed incredibly and&nbsp;
sometimes they fail gracelessly.

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:51.040
So, be excited, these emergent&nbsp;
capabilities are indeed impressive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:53.280
coming from a “slightly conscious”

00:13:53.280 --> 00:14:01.280
I mean: “boring language model”. But also, keep&nbsp;
in mind that there are experiments in the paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:06.080
evaluating fairness and biases that&nbsp;
highlight several problems of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:10.400
model reinforcing biases and exhibiting toxicity.

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:17.280
There is a lot we do not understand about&nbsp;
these models, like why do the bad things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:26.080
such as toxicity happen? We do not even know why&nbsp;
the good things happen! Ok, scale enables the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:33.440
emergence of some key capabilities in the model,&nbsp;
like we have seen with PaLM and logical reasoning.

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:39.840
But in the open questions of the paper, we can&nbsp;
also see a discussion about a possible alternative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:46.320
to sheer model scale, which is the effect of&nbsp;
having lots of high quality training data.

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:57.040
So high quality data could be an alternative to&nbsp;
large parameter numbers. Or this could also mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.040 --> 00:15:06.480
that PaLM with its 540 billion parameters would&nbsp;
learn even more from more high-quality data.

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:17.760
So what do you think about this paper? On the&nbsp;
one hand the model shows impressive capabilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:23.120
but on the other hand, it’s boring, there&nbsp;
is no architectural innovation in it.

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:32.320
But you can imagine that while we did not see the&nbsp;
model innovation yet, now that Google knows how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:42.560
train these things efficiently on 6144 TPU chips&nbsp;
at once, we will see more research and innovation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:47.760
with large models on text, but also&nbsp;
on images, video, audio, you name it.

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:57.840
We’re living in exciting times and I do not even&nbsp;
start to say how a little PhD student feels about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:04.000
this. How do you feel about realizing over&nbsp;
and over again that Sutton’s bitter lesson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:10.560
is really a thing and scaling things up is&nbsp;
the way to go? Or at least one of the ways.

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:21.840
See you next time! Okay, bye!

